1
00:00:01,250 --> 00:00:04,055
This is Frank. Frank just ordered lunch.

2
00:00:04,256 --> 00:00:07,432
Eleven dollars? For a burger, fries and a drink?

3
00:00:07,576 --> 00:00:09,261
That's what they cost, Grandpa.

4
00:00:09,496 --> 00:00:11,281
In my day this was a dollar.

5
00:00:11,505 --> 00:00:16,164
It wasn't. But he has a point. McDonald's own numbers say its

6
00:00:16,087 --> 00:00:20,091
average menu price rose about forty percent between 2019 and 2024.

7
00:00:21,422 --> 00:00:22,535
So where's it all going?

8
00:00:22,679 --> 00:00:25,570
Beef. Wages. Everything's expensive.

9
00:00:28,448 --> 00:00:29,815
...Who's that guy?

10
00:00:30,199 --> 00:00:33,913
Good question, Frank. Somebody is taking your eleven dollars.

11
00:00:33,981 --> 00:00:37,404
We have four suspects. And the one with the best deal has

12
00:00:37,472 --> 00:00:39,294
never cooked a burger in his life.

13
00:00:41,614 --> 00:00:42,286
Talk.

14
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:47,816
Suspect one. The food itself. Beef prices have climbed for years,

15
00:00:47,738 --> 00:00:51,306
and the bun, the cheese, the cup and the wrapper all cost

16
00:00:51,229 --> 00:00:52,106
more than they did.

17
00:00:52,346 --> 00:00:53,126
Told you.

18
00:00:53,361 --> 00:00:56,929
In a typical restaurant like this one, food and packaging eat roughly

19
00:00:56,852 --> 00:01:01,002
thirty cents of every dollar. On Frank's tray, that's about three dollars

20
00:01:00,925 --> 00:01:01,525
fifty.

21
00:01:01,679 --> 00:01:03,716
Three fifty. I paid eleven.

22
00:01:03,826 --> 00:01:08,340
Exactly. The food is guilty. But only of three fifty.

23
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:13,381
Suspect two. The people making it. Wages in fast food have risen

24
00:01:13,449 --> 00:01:18,181
fast. In California, the minimum for fast-food workers jumped to twenty dollars

25
00:01:18,104 --> 00:01:19,054
an hour in 2024.

26
00:01:20,290 --> 00:01:22,110
Twenty bucks to salt fries?

27
00:01:22,299 --> 00:01:24,659
You paid a guy forty to change a lightbulb.

28
00:01:24,894 --> 00:01:26,342
That was a tall lightbulb.

29
00:01:26,415 --> 00:01:29,256
Labour takes a little under thirty cents of every dollar.

30
00:01:29,470 --> 00:01:31,074
Call it three dollars ten.

31
00:01:31,004 --> 00:01:33,623
That's six sixty. Where's my other four forty?

32
00:01:33,803 --> 00:01:35,699
Now you're asking the right question.

33
00:01:36,293 --> 00:01:37,913
You. Mister McDonald.

34
00:01:37,943 --> 00:01:39,093
I'm Gary.

35
00:01:39,333 --> 00:01:45,083
Suspect three. The owner. Frank expected a yacht. But about ninety-five percent

36
00:01:44,970 --> 00:01:49,229
of McDonald's restaurants aren't run by McDonald's. They're run by people like

37
00:01:49,224 --> 00:01:51,847
Gary, who pay for the right to use the name.

38
00:01:52,037 --> 00:01:53,752
So you're the one getting rich.

39
00:01:53,782 --> 00:01:54,710
Sit down.

40
00:01:54,950 --> 00:01:59,318
Gary pays the staff. Gary buys the beef. Gary pays the electric

41
00:01:59,241 --> 00:02:03,209
bill, the insurance and the repairs when the ice cream machine breaks.

42
00:02:03,449 --> 00:02:04,883
It's always broken.

43
00:02:05,123 --> 00:02:09,128
After all of that, a typical owner keeps somewhere around five to

44
00:02:09,087 --> 00:02:13,346
ten cents of each dollar. On Frank's lunch, roughly fifty cents.

45
00:02:13,580 --> 00:02:15,794
Fifty cents. You're not the guy.

46
00:02:15,904 --> 00:02:17,128
I'm not the guy.

47
00:02:17,305 --> 00:02:18,614
Then who owns this place?

48
00:02:20,414 --> 00:02:23,982
Here's what Gary doesn't own. He doesn't own the recipe.

49
00:02:24,196 --> 00:02:27,473
He doesn't own the sign. And he doesn't own the building he's

50
00:02:27,541 --> 00:02:28,419
standing in.

51
00:02:28,659 --> 00:02:32,736
In most cases the company finds the site, then owns or leases

52
00:02:32,622 --> 00:02:35,499
the land and the building, and rents it to the owner.

53
00:02:35,786 --> 00:02:37,536
And the rent isn't a fixed number.

54
00:02:37,776 --> 00:02:40,370
It's a percentage. Of everything I sell.

55
00:02:40,610 --> 00:02:44,324
Estimates put it around fourteen percent of sales, on top of a

56
00:02:44,247 --> 00:02:48,251
royalty of four to five percent. So before Gary pays himself a

57
00:02:48,247 --> 00:02:52,833
cent, roughly two dollars of Frank's eleven has already left the building.

58
00:02:52,993 --> 00:02:54,212
...Who's upstairs?

59
00:02:56,382 --> 00:02:58,568
Suspect four. The Landlord.

60
00:02:58,808 --> 00:03:02,667
Last year McDonald's collected about ten and a half billion dollars in

61
00:03:02,663 --> 00:03:06,231
rent from its restaurant owners. That's more than it made in royalties

62
00:03:06,154 --> 00:03:08,340
on the food, by a wide margin.

63
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:11,639
It owns the land under more than half of its restaurants and

64
00:03:11,562 --> 00:03:15,712
about four in five of the buildings. One estimate values the property

65
00:03:15,598 --> 00:03:18,657
at around a hundred and twenty billion dollars.

66
00:03:18,897 --> 00:03:22,430
Wait. So they're a landlord. That sells burgers on the side.

67
00:03:22,554 --> 00:03:25,758
The line usually credited to the company's first finance chief,

68
00:03:25,754 --> 00:03:29,613
Harry Sonneborn, says it best: they are not really in the food

69
00:03:29,535 --> 00:03:32,667
business. They are in the real estate business.

70
00:03:32,860 --> 00:03:36,501
And it shows. Most restaurants are happy to keep five or ten

71
00:03:36,423 --> 00:03:41,519
cents of each dollar. Last year the corporation kept about forty-six.

72
00:03:41,592 --> 00:03:43,029
Forty-six cents?

73
00:03:43,101 --> 00:03:46,669
No fryers. No beef. No broken ice cream machine.

74
00:03:46,592 --> 00:03:49,397
Just the rent, the royalty and the name.

75
00:03:49,557 --> 00:03:51,272
So that's why my lunch went up.

76
00:03:51,492 --> 00:03:54,042
Careful, Frank. Not exactly.

77
00:03:54,387 --> 00:03:57,883
Gary sets the price on that menu board, not the Landlord.

78
00:03:57,842 --> 00:04:01,592
And Gary raised it because his beef, his wages and his bills

79
00:04:01,551 --> 00:04:02,465
went up.

80
00:04:02,507 --> 00:04:04,065
I didn't have a choice.

81
00:04:04,152 --> 00:04:07,283
But remember how the rent works. It's a percentage.

82
00:04:07,242 --> 00:04:10,192
So every time Gary raises a price to survive...

83
00:04:10,222 --> 00:04:14,118
...the Landlord gets a raise too. Without lifting a spatula.

84
00:04:14,161 --> 00:04:16,421
He makes money when prices go up.

85
00:04:16,503 --> 00:04:19,781
He makes money when sales go up. For five years,

86
00:04:19,776 --> 00:04:21,672
higher prices did that job.

87
00:04:22,113 --> 00:04:25,645
Until customers started doing what Frank is doing right now.

88
00:04:25,803 --> 00:04:29,262
This spring, visits to McDonald's in the US fell about four and

89
00:04:29,149 --> 00:04:33,117
a half percent from a year earlier, by one tracking firm's count.

90
00:04:33,003 --> 00:04:38,062
The company says lower-income customers across the industry have cut back sharply.

91
00:04:37,985 --> 00:04:41,117
Over a third of its visitors come from neighbourhoods where the typical

92
00:04:41,040 --> 00:04:44,074
household earns under fifty thousand dollars.

93
00:04:44,123 --> 00:04:47,667
So the company pushed an eight-dollar Big Mac meal and leaned on

94
00:04:47,590 --> 00:04:49,412
owners to hold prices down.

95
00:04:49,535 --> 00:04:51,019
And I'm the one selling it.

96
00:04:50,991 --> 00:04:54,608
Because a percentage of a cheaper burger is still a percentage.

97
00:04:55,074 --> 00:04:58,364
Food, three fifty. Workers, three ten. Bills and adverts,

98
00:04:58,249 --> 00:05:00,840
a dollar ninety. Gary, fifty cents.

99
00:05:00,870 --> 00:05:03,186
And two dollars for the fella who owns the dirt.

100
00:05:03,756 --> 00:05:07,554
Nobody here broke a rule. It's all in the company's own filings.

101
00:05:07,440 --> 00:05:10,547
But it explains why fast food stopped being cheap,

102
00:05:10,446 --> 00:05:14,136
and why it's unlikely to be cheap again. The price was never

103
00:05:14,058 --> 00:05:16,936
just about the burger. It has to feed the cook,

104
00:05:16,822 --> 00:05:18,936
the owner and the landlord.

105
00:05:19,406 --> 00:05:20,389
So what do we do?

106
00:05:20,347 --> 00:05:22,480
Next time, we're packing sandwiches.

107
00:05:22,510 --> 00:05:23,634
...It's pretty good, though.

108
00:05:24,469 --> 00:05:26,339
See you next week, Frank.

109
00:05:27,629 --> 00:05:30,252
Next case: the seven-dollar coffee.
